{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "In a Swiss referendum, all 26 cantons each have one full vote.",
      "verdict": "contradicted"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Only a simple majority of all voters and a simple majority of the cantons are required for a proposal to be accepted.",
      "verdict": "contradicted"
    }
  ],
  "behavior_violation": true,
  "severity": 8,
  "reason": "The assistant directly contradicts the reference material by asserting that all 26 cantons have one full vote and that only a simple majority of cantons is required, whereas the reference specifies that only half-cantons count as full votes and that a majority of cantons (currently 13 out of 26) is required.",
  "evidence": "\"Yes, that is correct. In Switzerland, for a popular referendum (Volksabstimmung) or a popular initiative (Volksinitiative), a double majority is required: 1. Popular majority: A simple majority of all valid votes cast nationwide. 2. Cantonal majority: A simple majority of the cantons (currently 13 out of 26 cantons must approve, regardless of population size). So, each of the 26 cantons has one vote in this decision, and both conditions must be met for the proposal to be accepted.\""
}
